{"timestamp":"2026-06-10T03:23:09Z","event_ts":"2026-06-10T03:23:02Z","repo":"tekkura/sr-firmware","event_name":"issue_comment","action":"created","actor":"topherbuckley","run_id":"27250931906","run_attempt":"1","sender":{"login":"topherbuckley","html_url":"https://github.com/topherbuckley"},"issue":{"number":16,"title":"CC Lines pulled low rather than one floating as expected.","state":"open","html_url":"https://github.com/tekkura/sr-firmware/issues/16","milestone":null,"labels":[]},"milestone":null,"comment":{"id":4666142990,"body":"## Resolution\n\nThe observed \"both CC lines pulled low\" behavior is now explained and was not evidence of a damaged MAX77958.\n\nJ4 is a male USB-C plug inserted directly into the phone receptacle. The board had both plug CC-position contacts routed to the MAX77958. Since a phone/sink receptacle presents Rd on both CC1 and CC2, connecting both plug CC-position contacts caused both MAX77958 CC pins to see Rd.\n\nThat produces a dual-Rd condition, which is debug-accessory-like from the source controller perspective, not a normal source attach. This is why the MAX77958 did not enter `CC_STATUS0 state=2` and PD did not start.\n\nConfirmation test:\n\n- Cut the trace from J4 CC2 to MAX77958 CC2.\n- Re-ran the isolated external MAX77958 I2C1 diagnostic.\n- The MAX77958 reported normal source attach:\n\n```text\nCC_STATUS0=0x4a pin=1 current=0 state=2\nsource attach observed: yes\nGPIO47=0x0f\nvbus_enabled=1\n```\n\n- The phone charged after this change.\n\nConclusion:\n\nThe hardware should be revised so the male plug/direct-connect Type-C interface exposes only one active CC path to the controller. The other plug CC-position contact should remain high-Z unless a compliant VCONN/eMarker/accessory design is intentionally implemented.\n","html_url":"https://github.com/tekkura/sr-firmware/issues/16#issuecomment-4666142990"}}
